##:
io/contracts -- Reader, Writer and Seek: what a handle can do, named apart from what a
handle IS.

The module ships as bundled .sushi source and is merged as a compilation unit when
imported (`use <io/contracts>`). It declares three perks and nothing else: no struct, no
function, no constant. A consumer writes `@(R: Reader)` and takes a File, a TcpStream or
anything later that satisfies the contract.

Every contract method answers IoError, and it has to: a perk contract carries ONE
signature, and there is no Self type and no associated type, so a method cannot answer
FileError on a File and NetError on a TcpStream. That is why the concrete constructors keep
the detailed enum -- open() and connect() -- while the data path is one channel a
generic can propagate with ??.

Limitations: a perk has no default implementations, so a convenience above these methods is
a free generic function rather than a provided method. A perk cannot carry a type
parameter, so there is no generic Reader@(T).
:##

public use <io/error>

##:
A handle bytes can be read from.

read() answers text and read_bytes() answers bytes. They take the same bound and differ
only in what they hand back. Both RETURN a fresh value rather than filling a buffer the
caller owns. For read() that is forced: a string carries a size and no capacity, so a text
read has nowhere to write into. For read_bytes() it was measured: the one allocation a
returned array costs is a few percent of the read itself, and a fill-into form could not
remove it, because a u8[] crosses to the descriptor primitive by value and the primitive
cannot set the caller's length.

The bound counts BYTES, and one read may therefore split a multi-byte sequence. The
caller's answer is to accumulate bytes and convert once, the way read_all() and
read_line() do; converting each chunk on its own cuts the character in two. A character
bound would need a character index the string layer does not have.

An EMPTY answer means end of input, which is what lets a caller loop until the answer is
empty. A short answer is not an end: a pipe hands over whatever has arrived.
:##
public perk Reader:
    ##:
    Reads what one read gives, as text.

    - Parameter max: The most bytes to take in this one read.
    - Returns: The text that arrived, empty at end of input.
    - Errors: IoError, when the read failed.
    :##
    fn read(poke self, i32 max) string | IoError

    ##:
    Reads what one read gives, as bytes.

    - Parameter max: The most bytes to take in this one read.
    - Returns: The bytes that arrived, empty at end of input.
    - Errors: IoError, when the read failed.
    :##
    fn read_bytes(poke self, i32 max) u8[] | IoError

##:
A handle bytes can be written to.

Both writes answer nothing rather than a count, and both write EVERYTHING: the descriptor
primitives loop past a short write, so there is no partial answer to report. A caller that
needs to know how much one attempt took wants the concrete method on the handle -- a
socket's partial write is information, and a file's is not.
:##
public perk Writer:
    ##:
    Writes text, and does not stop until every byte has gone.

    - Parameter data: The text to write.
    - Returns: Nothing.
    - Errors: IoError, when the write failed.
    :##
    fn write(poke self, string data) ~ | IoError

    ##:
    Writes bytes, and does not stop until every one has gone.

    - Parameter data: The bytes to write. The buffer stays the caller's.
    - Returns: Nothing.
    - Errors: IoError, when the write failed.
    :##
    fn write_bytes(poke self, u8[] data) ~ | IoError

    ##:
    Makes sure what was written has left this layer.

    A descriptor is not buffered, so a handle's flush is a successful no-op. The buffered
    writer is where the call earns its name.

    - Returns: Nothing.
    - Errors: IoError, when the flush failed.
    :##
    fn flush(poke self) ~ | IoError

##:
A handle whose read and write position can be moved.

A file satisfies this and a socket does not: a stream has no position to move, and asking
for the perk is how a caller says it needs one.
:##
public perk Seek:
    ##:
    Moves the position, and answers where it landed.

    - Parameter offset: How far to move, which may be negative.
    - Parameter origin: Which end to count from.
    - Returns: The new position, in bytes from the start.
    - Errors: IoError, when the handle cannot seek -- a pipe cannot.
    :##
    fn seek(poke self, i64 offset, SeekFrom origin) i64 | IoError
